Closes #1592. Part of #1579.

## In plain words

When a thread runs in the cloud, its agent uses a cloud browser. The web app already shows that browser live in the chat's **Browser** tab (#1598), but the desktop app could not. The view is a WebSocket that signs in with a bearer token, and a page's `WebSocket` cannot send one. Now the desktop host opens the socket itself and passes it to the page. The same `CloudBrowserView` component the web uses then draws it in the desktop:

- in a chat's inspector **Browser** tab, as on the web;
- in a **Local work** thread that runs in the cloud, in the thread's own **Browser** tab, where the local page shows while the thread runs on this computer.

The toolbar, the pill (**Agent**, **You**, **Ended**), **Take over**, input on the canvas and **Hand back** with a note all work as on the web, because they are the same component.

## Changes

**Host (`apps/elitea-desktop/src-tauri`)**
- New `src/view_socket.rs`, with two commands:
  - `browser_view_open {id, url, events}` opens the view on an HTTP/1.1 client (`SharedHttp::upgrade_client`). That client has the same TLS roots as the pooled client but no h2, because an upgrade cannot run over h2. The request sends `Authorization: Bearer`, `X-Client-Version` and no `Origin`. The token never crosses the IPC.
  - `browser_view_send {id, messages, close}` carries the page's text messages in order, then its close.
- The URL must be the view route `…/browser_sessions/{id}/view` of the connected deployment (`HttpScope`). Userinfo, a query or a fragment is refused.
- Events go to the page on a Tauri `Channel`:
  - open and text as small JSON;
  - **JPEG frames as the raw bytes of the channel message, never base64.** Tauri hands a raw body of 1 KiB or more to the page as an `ArrayBuffer` over its IPC protocol, in one copy;
  - a close event that carries the **HTTP status of a refused upgrade**: 404, 410, 429, 502, 503; 401 after one refresh and one retry; 426 for a build that is too old.
- A 401 refreshes once through `AuthService::refresh_from`. That skips the exchange if another refresh already replaced the token, so rotating refresh tokens are not spent twice.
- Bounds:
  - 8 views at once;
  - a deployment message is at most 16 MiB;
  - one send is at most 256 messages of 8 KiB each;
  - the upgrade must answer within 30 s;
  - a close waits 5 s for the answer;
  - a page load closes every socket of the old page.
- `tokio-tungstenite` 0.30 is added with `handshake` only, for the framing. It brings no TLS and no connector. It is already in the platform's locks (elitea-worker-rust); the new lock entries are `tokio-tungstenite`, `tungstenite` and `sha1` 0.11.
- The capability gains exactly `allow-browser-view-open` and `allow-browser-view-send`. `build.rs` and `lib.rs` register them, which `config_tests` checks.

**Web (`apps/elitea-web`)**
- New `shared/desktop/hostViewSocket.ts`, a `BrowserViewSocket` over the host's channel:
  - it puts out-of-order events back in order;
  - it sends one host call at a time, so input stays in order;
  - it releases the channel callback when Tauri ends the channel.
- `createHostTransport` registers it as `NativeTransport.createWebSocket` (wired in `launchApp`). `browserViewAvailable()` is now true on desktop, so the chat's Browser tab shows there too.
- `shared/api/browserView/client.ts` reads `socket.refusedStatus` (a new optional field on `BrowserViewSocket`), so the desktop sends no diagnosing GET. A 426 on the upgrade becomes the new failure `upgrade_required`, which is not retried, says "Update the app…", and raises the shell's update notice (`onUpgradeRequired`). The browser path is unchanged: there, 426 on the diagnosing GET still means "transient".
- `pages/workspace/ThreadBrowserTab.tsx` and `cloudBrowserTab.ts`: a Local work thread away from this computer (`handoff.offLaptop`) shows the session of the newest answer that used the browser. It reads the cloud copy's transcript with the chat's `cloudBrowserSessionOf`, in the same `browser` inspector tab. Before a run uses the browser there is no tab.

